response:
{
  "answer": "Yes",
  "reasoning": "If you witness someone being struck hard enough to cause a cut and swelling, that is potentially a criminal assault and/or child abuse and should be reported to authorities. If the injured person is your son or daughter (the victim), report immediately to the police and/or your local child protective services so they can ensure safety and get medical care. If your son or daughter is the one delivering the blows (the aggressor), you should still contact police/juvenile authorities and seek help for them — violent conduct that causes injury may require intervention and carries legal consequences. Mandatory reporting rules vary by jurisdiction and by whether you are a mandated reporter (teacher, medical professional, etc.), but the safety of the injured person and preventing further harm take priority. If there is immediate danger, call emergency services; preserve any evidence and document what you saw. If unsure, contact local authorities or a lawyer for guidance on the local legal obligations."
}

